{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787774737,"nonce":"agAyr2J/D9Fv3mvJRcTOnc8Gq+A=","title":"feat(connections): /v1/connections read API + GitHub observe surface (ADR-0060 slice 3)","message":"\u003e Imported from pull request #77 (MERGED)\n\u003e Branch: `feat/adr-0060-connections-read` → `main`\n\u003e Created: 2026-07-25T17:16:23Z · Merged: 2026-07-25T17:58:21Z\n\nThird slice of ADR-0060. Closes git-bug `998f20b`.\n\n**Stacked on #75 → #74.** Review order: 74, 75, then this.\n\nA `ForgeConnection` is what makes a repo a Project — and there was no way to\nsee one. If the App's credential went missing (a DB restored without its\nsecrets, a reseed that never happened) **nothing said so** until a run failed\nat the first forge call.\n\n`GET /v1/connections` — each connection with its bound Projects, its\ncredential handle and **whether that handle resolves**, and the last recorded\ndelivery for its forge kind. `Administer` on the org, because a connection\nspans every Project it serves.\n\n`POST /v1/connections/{id}/resync` — ask the forge which repos this credential\nreaches and bind any that are missing. This is the fix for the one failure\nGitHub leaves Scarab able to repair itself: a dropped\n`installation_repositories` delivery leaving a covered repo with no Project.\n\nUI: a **Forge connections** section above Org Secrets — kind, base URL,\ncredential health, last delivery, bound-Project chips, **Manage on GitHub →**,\nand **Re-sync**.\n\nGitHub stays **observe-only by construction**: installing the App *is*\nregistration, and there is no API for Scarab to install it or change repo\nselection — a create form could only lie.\n\n1. **The health readout must not leak what it reports on.** The DTO carries\n   `credential_present: bool` and the handle, never the material. A test\n   asserts the whole payload cannot contain the seeded PEM.\n2. **Re-sync only binds, never unbinds.** Environments, secrets and RBAC hang\n   off a Project, so letting one failed API page decide to *remove* one would\n   let a transient error destroy governance. `confirmed` reports the overlap so\n   a stale binding stays visible; removal stays a human act.\n3. **`Unsupported` ≠ empty.** New `ForgePort::list_accessible_repos` defaults to\n   `ForgeError::Unsupported`, not `Ok(vec![])` — \"cannot enumerate\" and\n   \"reaches nothing\" are different answers, and conflating them would make a\n   re-sync look like it succeeded (and, if re-sync ever unbound, like it should\n   delete everything). The endpoint answers **501**.\n\nGitHub implements it over `/app/installations` → `/installation/repositories`,\nwith its own pagination loop: that endpoint answers with an *object*, which\n`get_paginated` treats as terminal — it would have silently capped at 100.\n\n`crates/scarab-server/tests/connections.rs` — 6 tests: bound Projects + health\nwith **no credential leak** · a missing credential is reported not hidden ·\n`Administer` enforced on both endpoints (Org-admin yes, Viewer 403) · re-sync\nbinds the missed repo, it resolves as a Project and appears on `/v1/repos`,\nand a second run is idempotent · **re-sync never unbinds what the forge omits**\n· an adapter that cannot enumerate answers 501, and an unknown connection 404.\n\nFull workspace suite **578 passed** with `SCARAB_TEST_REQUIRE_PG=1` · `npm\ntest` 72 passed · typecheck clean · rendered in mock mode (screenshot-verified\nsection ordering, chips, and both actions).\n\nSlice 4 (`8b3cf79`) adds the Forgejo create path + credential write-through;\nslice 5 (`b48df22`) is the payoff — Forgejo repo binding and webhook\nregistration, reusing this slice's `list_accessible_repos` for the pick-list.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","files":null}]}